{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_013kpXXSKEyCLsbkSzVG3QQ5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The test says:\n```\nlet ipv4_encoded_size = 1 + 4 + 2; // family(varint) + 4-byte addr + 2-byte port\nlet ipv6_encoded_size = 1 + 16 + 2; // family(varint) + 16-byte addr + 2-byte port\n```\n\nSo for IPv4, the family varint is 1 byte. That means the family value is < 64 (since varint encoding: if value < 64, it's 1 byte). AF_INET = 2 fits. For IPv6, also 1 byte, so the family must be < 64. \n\nAF_INET6 = 10 on Linux or 30 on macOS, both fit in 1 byte. I'll go with the Linux values (2 for IPv4, 10 for IPv6) since that's what picoquic primarily uses and they're the standard network constants. The test only checks roundtrip consistency anyway, so as long as write and read match, I'm good.\n\nNow I'm implementing the bytestream.rs file with helper methods for accessing the underlying buffer data.\n\nWriting helper methods...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3786,"cache_read_input_tokens":134967,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3786},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0ef721e-e3c5-4ce3-b9f2-772ba297c48d","uuid":"4a8cafc7-c792-4bb3-b588-ff380413e573"}
