{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M7cYgh1LLRUuDRkSMQzvAv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/config.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Demo-application–facing configuration plumbing for quic-core:\n4\t//! the [`Config`] bag of CLI-derived options, the [`OptionId`]\n5\t//! tag identifying each option, and [`Config::create_and_configure`],\n6\t//! the one-shot constructor that turns a populated config into a\n7\t//! fully-wired [`Quic`].\n8\t//!\n9\t//! Phase 1: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n10\t//!\n11\t//! Pointer-shape decisions for the config struct were guided by\n12\t//! reading `quic/config.c`:\n13\t//!\n14\t//! * Every `char const*` string field that\n15\t//!   `config_clear` `free`s is owned in C (allocated via\n16\t//!   `config_set_string_param`'s `malloc` + `memcpy`).  These map\n17\t//!   to `Option<String>`; `None` substitutes for the C `NULL`\n18\t//!   sentinel that all callers test before use.\n19\t//! * `multipath_alt_config: *mut char` is owned the same way and\n20\t//!   becomes `Option<String>`.\n21\t//! * `ech_target: uint8_t*` plus `ech_target_len` is owned by the\n22\t//!   config (`config_clear` frees it after the base64\n23\t//!   decode in `option_ECH_client`); it becomes\n24\t//!   `Option<Vec<u8>>` with the length implicit in the vector.\n25\t//! * `ticket_encryption_key: const uint8_t*` is *borrowed* in C —\n26\t//!   `config_clear` does not free it.  Phase 1 stores an\n27\t//!   owned `Option<Vec<u8>>` for safety; in v1 only the demo apps\n28\t//!   set this field, and they can supply an owned buffer.  Phase 3\n29\t//!   may revisit if a caller actually relies on aliasing.  The\n30\t//!   paired `ticket_encryption_key_length` field is dropped — its\n31\t//!   value is always implicit in the Vec length.\n32\t//! * Single-bit `unsigned int : 1` flag bitfields (`use_long_log`,\n33\t//!   `do_retry`, …) collapse to individual `bool` fields.  Every C\n34\t//!   call site reads/writes one bit at a time as a Boolean\n35\t//!   (`config->do_retry = 1`), so the\n36\t//!   integer-with-mask-and-shift translation buys nothing here and\n37\t//!   `bitflags!` is overkill for an unrelated set of flags.\n38\t//! * Bitfield `enable_sslkeylog` is gated by\n39\t//!   `#ifndef WITHOUT_SSLKEYLOG` in C.  v1 follows the\n40\t//!   canonical build (the macro is undefined), so the field is\n41\t//!   always present in Rust; a `cfg`-gated variant lands when the\n42\t//!   build options are translated.\n43\t\n44\tuse crate::Error;\n45\tuse crate::Instant;\n46\tuse crate::{LossbitVersion, Quic, SpinbitVersion, StreamDataCallback};\n47\t\n48\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n49\t// Option identifiers.\n50\t\n51\t/// One identifier per CLI / API option understood by [`Config`].\n52\t///\n53\t/// Mirrors the C `picoquic_option_enum_t`; variant order is\n54\t/// load-bearing — the option dispatch table in `quic/config.c`\n55\t/// indexes by variant — so the discriminants do not get reordered.\n56\t///\n57\t/// [`OptionId::SslKeyLog`] is unconditional here even though the C\n58\t/// enum gates it on `#ifndef WITHOUT_SSLKEYLOG`, matching the\n59\t/// canonical-build behavior; see the module-level docs for the\n60\t/// rationale.\n61\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n62\tpub enum OptionId {\n63\t    Cert,\n64\t    Key,\n65\t    ServerPort,\n66\t    ProposedVersion,\n67\t    OutDir,\n68\t    WwwDir,\n69\t    MaxConnections,\n70\t    DoRetry,\n71\t    InitialRandom,\n72\t    ResetSeed,\n73\t    DisablePortBlocking,\n74\t    SolutionDir,\n75\t    CcAlgo,\n76\t    CcOption,\n77\t    Spinbit,\n78\t    Lossbit,\n79\t    Multipath,\n80\t    DestIf,\n81\t    CipherSuite,\n82\t    InitCnxId,\n83\t    LogFile,\n84\t    LongLog,\n85\t    BinlogDir,\n86\t    QlogDir,\n87\t    MtuMax,\n88\t    Sni,\n89\t    Alpn,\n90\t    RootTrustFile,\n91\t    ForceZeroShare,\n92\t    CnxIdLength,\n93\t    NoDisk,\n94\t    IdleTimeout,\n95\t    LargeClientHello,\n96\t    TicketFileName,\n97\t    TokenFileName,\n98\t    SocketBufferSize,\n99\t    PerformanceLog,\n100\t    PreemptiveRepeat,\n101\t    VersionUpgrade,\n102\t    NoGso,\n103\t    BdpFrame,\n104\t    CwinMax,\n105\t    SslKeyLog,\n106\t    AddressDiscovery,\n107\t    EchServer,\n108\t    EchClient,\n109\t    EchInit,\n110\t    FlowControlMax,\n111\t    PreferredV4,\n112\t    PreferredV6,\n113\t    Help,\n114\t}\n115\t\n116\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n117\t// Configuration struct.\n118\t\n119\t/// Configuration bag for a QUIC context, populated from CLI flags\n120\t/// (via [`Config::command_line`]) or directly (via\n121\t/// [`Config::set_option`]) and consumed by\n122\t/// [`Config::create_and_configure`].\n123\t///\n124\t/// Mirrors the C `picoquic_quic_config_t` field-for-field;\n125\t/// `repr(C)` is dropped because the struct never crosses an\n126\t/// external boundary.  See the module-level docs for the\n127\t/// pointer-shape rationale.\n128\t///\n129\t/// `Default` produces the documented C defaults:\n130\t/// `nb_connections = 256`, `connection_id_length = -1`, `cwin_max =\n131\t/// u64::MAX`, idle timeout from `MICROSEC_HANDSHAKE_MAX`, etc.  This\n132\t/// folds the C `picoquic_config_init` step into the constructor —\n133\t/// the C `config_clear` (which `free`s every owned string) collapses\n134\t/// to `Drop`, and resetting a config to defaults is just\n135\t/// `*config = Config::default()`.\n136\t#[derive(Debug)]\n137\tpub struct Config {\n138\t    pub nb_connections: u32,\n139\t    pub solution_dir: Option<String>,\n140\t    pub server_cert_file: Option<String>,\n141\t    pub server_key_file: Option<String>,\n142\t    pub log_file: Option<String>,\n143\t    pub bin_dir: Option<String>,\n144\t    pub qlog_dir: Option<String>,\n145\t    pub performance_log: Option<String>,\n146\t    pub server_port: u16,\n147\t    pub local_port: u16,\n148\t    /// Whether the public port is shared with sibling threads\n149\t    /// (`SO_REUSEPORT`).  C: `int is_port_shared`, used as a\n150\t    /// Boolean flag.\n151\t    pub is_port_shared: bool,\n152\t    pub nb_threads: i32,\n153\t    pub dest_if: i32,\n154\t    pub mtu_max: i32,\n155\t    /// `-1` is the C \"unset\" sentinel applied by [`Self::init`];\n156\t    /// values `>= 0` set the connection-ID length explicitly.  Kept\n157\t    /// as `i32` rather than `Option<u8>` for source-level parity\n158\t    /// with the C body.\n159\t    pub connection_id_length: i32,\n160\t    pub idle_timeout: i32,\n161\t    pub socket_buffer_size: i32,\n162\t    pub cc_algo_id: Option<String>,\n163\t    pub cc_algo_option_string: Option<String>,\n164\t    pub connection_id_cbdata: Option<String>,\n165\t    pub spinbit_policy: SpinbitVersion,\n166\t    pub lossbit_policy: LossbitVersion,\n167\t    pub multipath_option: i32,\n168\t    pub multipath_alt_config: Option<String>,\n169\t    pub bdp_frame_option: i32,\n170\t    pub cwin_max: u64,\n171\t    pub address_discovery_mode: i32,\n172\t\n173\t    // Common flags.\n174\t    pub initial_random: u32,\n175\t    pub use_long_log: bool,\n176\t    pub do_preemptive_repeat: bool,\n177\t    pub do_not_use_gso: bool,\n178\t    pub disable_port_blocking: bool,\n179\t    /// Originally bitfield-gated by `#ifndef\n180\t    /// WITHOUT_SSLKEYLOG`; v1 follows the canonical build\n181\t    /// (always defined), so the field is unconditional here.\n182\t    pub enable_sslkeylog: bool,\n183\t\n184\t    // Server only.\n185\t    pub www_dir: Option<String>,\n186\t    pub reset_seed: [u8; 16],\n187\t    /// Borrowed in C (`config_clear` does not free it); owned\n188\t    /// `Vec<u8>` here for safety.  The C\n189\t    /// `ticket_encryption_key_length` field is dropped — its value\n190\t    /// is always `ticket_encryption_key.as_ref().map_or(0, Vec::len)`.\n191\t    pub ticket_encryption_key: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n192\t\n193\t    // Server flags.\n194\t    pub do_retry: bool,\n195\t    pub has_reset_seed: bool,\n196\t\n197\t    // Client only.\n198\t    pub ticket_file_name: Option<String>,\n199\t    pub token_file_name: Option<String>,\n200\t    pub sni: Option<String>,\n201\t    pub alpn: Option<String>,\n202\t    pub out_dir: Option<String>,\n203\t    pub root_trust_file: Option<String>,\n204\t    pub cipher_suite_id: i32,\n205\t    pub proposed_version: u32,\n206\t    pub desired_version: u32,\n207\t    pub force_zero_share: bool,\n208\t    pub no_disk: bool,\n209\t    pub large_client_hello: bool,\n210\t\n211\t    // ECH parameters for server.\n212\t    pub ech_key_file: Option<String>,\n213\t    pub ech_config_file: Option<String>,\n214\t    pub ech_public_name: Option<String>,\n215\t    /// ECH parameter for the client, base64-decoded.  Owned by\n216\t    /// the config — `config_clear` frees it in C.  The C\n217\t    /// `ech_target_len` field is dropped — its value is always\n218\t    /// `ech_target.as_ref().map_or(0, Vec::len)`.\n219\t    pub ech_target: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n220\t\n221\t    pub flow_control_max: u64,\n222\t\n223\t    // Preferred address, encoded as strings.\n224\t    pub preferred_address_v4: Option<String>,\n225\t    pub preferred_address_v6: Option<String>,\n226\t}\n227\t\n228\timpl Default for Config {\n229\t    /// Build a fresh config with the documented C defaults.  C:\n230\t    /// `picoquic_config_init` (which the C body invoked after a\n231\t    /// zero-initialising `memset`).\n232\t    fn default() -> Self {\n233\t        todo!()\n234\t    }\n235\t}\n236\t\n237\timpl Config {\n238\t    /// Apply one option, selected by `option`, to the config.\n239\t    /// C: `picoquic_config_set_option`.\n240\t    ///\n241\t    /// `value` is optional — flag-style options ([`OptionId::DoRetry`],\n242\t    /// [`OptionId::LongLog`], …) ignore it; value-style options\n243\t    /// require it and return `Err` when it is missing or malformed.\n244\t    ///\n245\t    /// The C signature returned `int` (`0` ↔ `Ok`, `-1` ↔ `Err`);\n246\t    /// mapped to `Result<(), Error>`.\n247\t    pub fn set_option(&mut self, _option: OptionId, _value: Option<&str>) -> Result<(), Error> {\n248\t        todo!()\n249\t    }\n250\t\n251\t    /// Dispatch one option from a single-character flag (`-x`).\n252\t    /// C: `picoquic_config_command_line`.\n253\t    ///\n254\t    /// `p_optind` advances past any extra arguments consumed beyond\n255\t    /// the inline `optarg`, mirroring the C in/out parameter so that\n256\t    /// an outer getopt-style loop stays in sync.\n257\t    ///\n258\t    /// `argv` is a borrowed slice of borrowed strings — the C caller\n259\t    /// (`first` etc.) owns the argument vector for the program\n260\t    /// lifetime, so a borrow is safe.  The C `int argc` parameter is\n261\t    /// dropped (implicit in `argv.len()`).\n262\t    pub fn command_line(\n263\t        &mut self,\n264\t        _opt: char,\n265\t        _p_optind: &mut usize,\n266\t        _argv: &[&str],\n267\t        _optarg: Option<&str>,\n268\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n269\t        todo!()\n270\t    }\n271\t\n272\t    /// Like [`Self::command_line`] but accepts both single-character\n273\t    /// (`-x`) and long-form (`--name`) option strings; the leading\n274\t    /// dashes are part of `opt_string`, matching the C contract.\n275\t    /// C: `picoquic_config_command_line_ex`.\n276\t    pub fn command_line_ex(\n277\t        &mut self,\n278\t        _opt_string: &str,\n279\t        _p_optind: &mut usize,\n280\t        _argv: &[&str],\n281\t        _optarg: Option<&str>,\n282\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n283\t        todo!()\n284\t    }\n285\t\n286\t    /// Build the getopt-style option string from the dispatch table\n287\t    /// in `config.c` — one letter per option, with `:` after each\n288\t    /// option that takes an argument.  C:\n289\t    /// `picoquic_config_option_letters`.\n290\t    ///\n291\t    /// The C signature wrote into a caller-supplied buffer\n292\t    /// (`option_string`, `string_max`) and reported the populated\n293\t    /// length via `*string_length`.  The Rust wrapper owns the\n294\t    /// buffer and returns it directly, so the buffer-too-small\n295\t    /// failure mode disappears.\n296\t    pub fn option_letters() -> String {\n297\t        todo!()\n298\t    }\n299\t\n300\t    /// Write the option help to a [`core::fmt::Write`] sink.\n301\t    /// C: `picoquic_config_usage_file`.\n302\t    ///\n303\t    /// The C parameter was `FILE*`; the Rust translation accepts\n304\t    /// any sink (a `String` buffer, the stdout/stderr handles under\n305\t    /// the `std` feature, or a custom writer) so the function\n306\t    /// stays `no_std`-friendly.\n307\t    pub fn write_usage(_w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write) {\n308\t        todo!()\n309\t    }\n310\t\n311\t    /// Print the option help to stderr.  C: `picoquic_config_usage`.\n312\t    ///\n313\t    /// Phase 3 will route this through `eprintln!` (std-only); for\n314\t    /// now it is just a `todo!()`.\n315\t    pub fn print_usage() {\n316\t        todo!()\n317\t    }\n318\t\n319\t    /// Parse a port-configuration string into `server_port`,\n320\t    /// `local_port`, `is_port_shared`, and `nb_threads`.\n321\t    /// C: `config_set_port` (internal, called from the `-p` option\n322\t    /// handler in `picoquic/config.c`).\n323\t    ///\n324\t    /// Accepted formats:\n325\t    /// - `\"\"` / `\"0\"` — clear all (port 0, not shared)\n326\t    /// - `\"4433\"` — server port only\n327\t    /// - `\"443:4434\"` — server:local\n328\t    /// - `\"S4433\"` — shared server port\n329\t    /// - `\"S443:4434*256\"` — shared server:local, 256 threads\n330\t    /// - `\"4433*7\"` — server port, 7 threads\n331\t    ///\n332\t    /// Returns `Err` when any token cannot be parsed or a port value\n333\t    /// exceeds 65535.\n334\t    pub fn set_port(&mut self, _port_string: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n335\t        todo!()\n336\t    }\n337\t\n338\t    /// Build a fully-configured QUIC context from this config plus\n339\t    /// an application-supplied callback.  C:\n340\t    /// `picoquic_create_and_configure`.\n341\t    ///\n342\t    /// Returns `None` when context creation fails (the C side\n343\t    /// returned `NULL`).\n344\t    ///\n345\t    /// Pointer-shape choices, derived from the C signature and the\n346\t    /// body in `config.c`:\n347\t    ///\n348\t    /// * `picoquic_quic_config_t* config` is consumed-borrowed\n349\t    ///   (`&mut self`): the body reads every field and may set up\n350\t    ///   downstream owned state, but it does not free the struct\n351\t    ///   itself — ownership stays with the caller.\n352\t    /// * `default_callback_fn` + `default_callback_ctx` collapse to\n353\t    ///   one `Option<Box<dyn StreamDataCallback>>` per the\n354\t    ///   function-pointers-map-to-traits rule, with the `void*`\n355\t    ///   context folded into the trait implementor's state.  `None`\n356\t    ///   matches the C \"no default callback\" case where the function\n357\t    ///   pointer was `NULL`.\n358\t    /// * `p_simulated_time: *mut u64` carries simulated wall time for\n359\t    ///   tests; the C QUIC context retains the pointer across calls,\n360\t    ///   so an `&mut u64` borrow expresses the contract directly.\n361\t    ///   Phase 3 may revisit (e.g. with a clock trait) once the QUIC\n362\t    ///   context type is real.\n363\t    pub fn create_and_configure(\n364\t        &mut self,\n365\t        _default_callback: Option<Box<dyn StreamDataCallback>>,\n366\t        _current_time: Instant,\n367\t        _p_simulated_time: Option<&mut u64>,\n368\t    ) -> Option<Box<Quic>> {\n369\t        todo!()\n370\t    }\n371\t}\n372\t\n373\t#[cfg(test)]\n374\tmod test {}\n375\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0ef721e-e3c5-4ce3-b9f2-772ba297c48d","uuid":"01fd536e-c815-45c8-8462-af0fe5b08ff3","timestamp":"2026-05-05T16:17:16.172Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/config.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/config.h`.\n//!\n//! Demo-application–facing configuration plumbing for quic-core:\n//! the [`Config`] bag of CLI-derived options, the [`OptionId`]\n//! tag identifying each option, and [`Config::create_and_configure`],\n//! the one-shot constructor that turns a populated config into a\n//! fully-wired [`Quic`].\n//!\n//! Phase 1: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n//!\n//! Pointer-shape decisions for the config struct were guided by\n//! reading `quic/config.c`:\n//!\n//! * Every `char const*` string field that\n//!   `config_clear` `free`s is owned in C (allocated via\n//!   `config_set_string_param`'s `malloc` + `memcpy`).  These map\n//!   to `Option<String>`; `None` substitutes for the C `NULL`\n//!   sentinel that all callers test before use.\n//! * `multipath_alt_config: *mut char` is owned the same way and\n//!   becomes `Option<String>`.\n//! * `ech_target: uint8_t*` plus `ech_target_len` is owned by the\n//!   config (`config_clear` frees it after the base64\n//!   decode in `option_ECH_client`); it becomes\n//!   `Option<Vec<u8>>` with the length implicit in the vector.\n//! * `ticket_encryption_key: const uint8_t*` is *borrowed* in C —\n//!   `config_clear` does not free it.  Phase 1 stores an\n//!   owned `Option<Vec<u8>>` for safety; in v1 only the demo apps\n//!   set this field, and they can supply an owned buffer.  Phase 3\n//!   may revisit if a caller actually relies on aliasing.  The\n//!   paired `ticket_encryption_key_length` field is dropped — its\n//!   value is always implicit in the Vec length.\n//! * Single-bit `unsigned int : 1` flag bitfields (`use_long_log`,\n//!   `do_retry`, …) collapse to individual `bool` fields.  Every C\n//!   call site reads/writes one bit at a time as a Boolean\n//!   (`config->do_retry = 1`), so the\n//!   integer-with-mask-and-shift translation buys nothing here and\n//!   `bitflags!` is overkill for an unrelated set of flags.\n//! * Bitfield `enable_sslkeylog` is gated by\n//!   `#ifndef WITHOUT_SSLKEYLOG` in C.  v1 follows the\n//!   canonical build (the macro is undefined), so the field is\n//!   always present in Rust; a `cfg`-gated variant lands when the\n//!   build options are translated.\n\nuse crate::Error;\nuse crate::Instant;\nuse crate::{LossbitVersion, Quic, SpinbitVersion, StreamDataCallback};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Option identifiers.\n\n/// One identifier per CLI / API option understood by [`Config`].\n///\n/// Mirrors the C `picoquic_option_enum_t`; variant order is\n/// load-bearing — the option dispatch table in `quic/config.c`\n/// indexes by variant — so the discriminants do not get reordered.\n///\n/// [`OptionId::SslKeyLog`] is unconditional here even though the C\n/// enum gates it on `#ifndef WITHOUT_SSLKEYLOG`, matching the\n/// canonical-build behavior; see the module-level docs for the\n/// rationale.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum OptionId {\n    Cert,\n    Key,\n    ServerPort,\n    ProposedVersion,\n    OutDir,\n    WwwDir,\n    MaxConnections,\n    DoRetry,\n    InitialRandom,\n    ResetSeed,\n    DisablePortBlocking,\n    SolutionDir,\n    CcAlgo,\n    CcOption,\n    Spinbit,\n    Lossbit,\n    Multipath,\n    DestIf,\n    CipherSuite,\n    InitCnxId,\n    LogFile,\n    LongLog,\n    BinlogDir,\n    QlogDir,\n    MtuMax,\n    Sni,\n    Alpn,\n    RootTrustFile,\n    ForceZeroShare,\n    CnxIdLength,\n    NoDisk,\n    IdleTimeout,\n    LargeClientHello,\n    TicketFileName,\n    TokenFileName,\n    SocketBufferSize,\n    PerformanceLog,\n    PreemptiveRepeat,\n    VersionUpgrade,\n    NoGso,\n    BdpFrame,\n    CwinMax,\n    SslKeyLog,\n    AddressDiscovery,\n    EchServer,\n    EchClient,\n    EchInit,\n    FlowControlMax,\n    PreferredV4,\n    PreferredV6,\n    Help,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Configuration struct.\n\n/// Configuration bag for a QUIC context, populated from CLI flags\n/// (via [`Config::command_line`]) or directly (via\n/// [`Config::set_option`]) and consumed by\n/// [`Config::create_and_configure`].\n///\n/// Mirrors the C `picoquic_quic_config_t` field-for-field;\n/// `repr(C)` is dropped because the struct never crosses an\n/// external boundary.  See the module-level docs for the\n/// pointer-shape rationale.\n///\n/// `Default` produces the documented C defaults:\n/// `nb_connections = 256`, `connection_id_length = -1`, `cwin_max =\n/// u64::MAX`, idle timeout from `MICROSEC_HANDSHAKE_MAX`, etc.  This\n/// folds the C `picoquic_config_init` step into the constructor —\n/// the C `config_clear` (which `free`s every owned string) collapses\n/// to `Drop`, and resetting a config to defaults is just\n/// `*config = Config::default()`.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Config {\n    pub nb_connections: u32,\n    pub solution_dir: Option<String>,\n    pub server_cert_file: Option<String>,\n    pub server_key_file: Option<String>,\n    pub log_file: Option<String>,\n    pub bin_dir: Option<String>,\n    pub qlog_dir: Option<String>,\n    pub performance_log: Option<String>,\n    pub server_port: u16,\n    pub local_port: u16,\n    /// Whether the public port is shared with sibling threads\n    /// (`SO_REUSEPORT`).  C: `int is_port_shared`, used as a\n    /// Boolean flag.\n    pub is_port_shared: bool,\n    pub nb_threads: i32,\n    pub dest_if: i32,\n    pub mtu_max: i32,\n    /// `-1` is the C \"unset\" sentinel applied by [`Self::init`];\n    /// values `>= 0` set the connection-ID length explicitly.  Kept\n    /// as `i32` rather than `Option<u8>` for source-level parity\n    /// with the C body.\n    pub connection_id_length: i32,\n    pub idle_timeout: i32,\n    pub socket_buffer_size: i32,\n    pub cc_algo_id: Option<String>,\n    pub cc_algo_option_string: Option<String>,\n    pub connection_id_cbdata: Option<String>,\n    pub spinbit_policy: SpinbitVersion,\n    pub lossbit_policy: LossbitVersion,\n    pub multipath_option: i32,\n    pub multipath_alt_config: Option<String>,\n    pub bdp_frame_option: i32,\n    pub cwin_max: u64,\n    pub address_discovery_mode: i32,\n\n    // Common flags.\n    pub initial_random: u32,\n    pub use_long_log: bool,\n    pub do_preemptive_repeat: bool,\n    pub do_not_use_gso: bool,\n    pub disable_port_blocking: bool,\n    /// Originally bitfield-gated by `#ifndef\n    /// WITHOUT_SSLKEYLOG`; v1 follows the canonical build\n    /// (always defined), so the field is unconditional here.\n    pub enable_sslkeylog: bool,\n\n    // Server only.\n    pub www_dir: Option<String>,\n    pub reset_seed: [u8; 16],\n    /// Borrowed in C (`config_clear` does not free it); owned\n    /// `Vec<u8>` here for safety.  The C\n    /// `ticket_encryption_key_length` field is dropped — its value\n    /// is always `ticket_encryption_key.as_ref().map_or(0, Vec::len)`.\n    pub ticket_encryption_key: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n\n    // Server flags.\n    pub do_retry: bool,\n    pub has_reset_seed: bool,\n\n    // Client only.\n    pub ticket_file_name: Option<String>,\n    pub token_file_name: Option<String>,\n    pub sni: Option<String>,\n    pub alpn: Option<String>,\n    pub out_dir: Option<String>,\n    pub root_trust_file: Option<String>,\n    pub cipher_suite_id: i32,\n    pub proposed_version: u32,\n    pub desired_version: u32,\n    pub force_zero_share: bool,\n    pub no_disk: bool,\n    pub large_client_hello: bool,\n\n    // ECH parameters for server.\n    pub ech_key_file: Option<String>,\n    pub ech_config_file: Option<String>,\n    pub ech_public_name: Option<String>,\n    /// ECH parameter for the client, base64-decoded.  Owned by\n    /// the config — `config_clear` frees it in C.  The C\n    /// `ech_target_len` field is dropped — its value is always\n    /// `ech_target.as_ref().map_or(0, Vec::len)`.\n    pub ech_target: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n\n    pub flow_control_max: u64,\n\n    // Preferred address, encoded as strings.\n    pub preferred_address_v4: Option<String>,\n    pub preferred_address_v6: Option<String>,\n}\n\nimpl Default for Config {\n    /// Build a fresh config with the documented C defaults.  C:\n    /// `picoquic_config_init` (which the C body invoked after a\n    /// zero-initialising `memset`).\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Config {\n    /// Apply one option, selected by `option`, to the config.\n    /// C: `picoquic_config_set_option`.\n    ///\n    /// `value` is optional — flag-style options ([`OptionId::DoRetry`],\n    /// [`OptionId::LongLog`], …) ignore it; value-style options\n    /// require it and return `Err` when it is missing or malformed.\n    ///\n    /// The C signature returned `int` (`0` ↔ `Ok`, `-1` ↔ `Err`);\n    /// mapped to `Result<(), Error>`.\n    pub fn set_option(&mut self, _option: OptionId, _value: Option<&str>) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Dispatch one option from a single-character flag (`-x`).\n    /// C: `picoquic_config_command_line`.\n    ///\n    /// `p_optind` advances past any extra arguments consumed beyond\n    /// the inline `optarg`, mirroring the C in/out parameter so that\n    /// an outer getopt-style loop stays in sync.\n    ///\n    /// `argv` is a borrowed slice of borrowed strings — the C caller\n    /// (`first` etc.) owns the argument vector for the program\n    /// lifetime, so a borrow is safe.  The C `int argc` parameter is\n    /// dropped (implicit in `argv.len()`).\n    pub fn command_line(\n        &mut self,\n        _opt: char,\n        _p_optind: &mut usize,\n        _argv: &[&str],\n        _optarg: Option<&str>,\n    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Like [`Self::command_line`] but accepts both single-character\n    /// (`-x`) and long-form (`--name`) option strings; the leading\n    /// dashes are part of `opt_string`, matching the C contract.\n    /// C: `picoquic_config_command_line_ex`.\n    pub fn command_line_ex(\n        &mut self,\n        _opt_string: &str,\n        _p_optind: &mut usize,\n        _argv: &[&str],\n        _optarg: Option<&str>,\n    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Build the getopt-style option string from the dispatch table\n    /// in `config.c` — one letter per option, with `:` after each\n    /// option that takes an argument.  C:\n    /// `picoquic_config_option_letters`.\n    ///\n    /// The C signature wrote into a caller-supplied buffer\n    /// (`option_string`, `string_max`) and reported the populated\n    /// length via `*string_length`.  The Rust wrapper owns the\n    /// buffer and returns it directly, so the buffer-too-small\n    /// failure mode disappears.\n    pub fn option_letters() -> String {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Write the option help to a [`core::fmt::Write`] sink.\n    /// C: `picoquic_config_usage_file`.\n    ///\n    /// The C parameter was `FILE*`; the Rust translation accepts\n    /// any sink (a `String` buffer, the stdout/stderr handles under\n    /// the `std` feature, or a custom writer) so the function\n    /// stays `no_std`-friendly.\n    pub fn write_usage(_w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Print the option help to stderr.  C: `picoquic_config_usage`.\n    ///\n    /// Phase 3 will route this through `eprintln!` (std-only); for\n    /// now it is just a `todo!()`.\n    pub fn print_usage() {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Parse a port-configuration string into `server_port`,\n    /// `local_port`, `is_port_shared`, and `nb_threads`.\n    /// C: `config_set_port` (internal, called from the `-p` option\n    /// handler in `picoquic/config.c`).\n    ///\n    /// Accepted formats:\n    /// - `\"\"` / `\"0\"` — clear all (port 0, not shared)\n    /// - `\"4433\"` — server port only\n    /// - `\"443:4434\"` — server:local\n    /// - `\"S4433\"` — shared server port\n    /// - `\"S443:4434*256\"` — shared server:local, 256 threads\n    /// - `\"4433*7\"` —