{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L433M7qtkeyxCC1kuA4Nik","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/lb.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Server-side support for the QUIC load-balancer draft\n4\t//! (<https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers/>):\n5\t//! parse the LB-supplied configuration string into a [`Config`],\n6\t//! then install a per-server CID-generation / -verification context\n7\t//! ([`ConnectionIdContext`]) on a [`Quic`] via the connection-ID\n8\t//! callback hook.\n9\t//!\n10\t//! ## API shape\n11\t//!\n12\t//! * [`Config::parse`] takes the LB string as `&str` and returns\n13\t//!   the populated config; the C `(char const* txt, size_t\n14\t//!   txt_length)` pair plus zeroed out-parameter collapse to a\n15\t//!   normal Rust constructor.\n16\t//! * [`Quic::set_lb_cid_config`] applies a `Config` to the QUIC\n17\t//!   context, installing the per-server CID context.\n18\t//! * [`Quic::clear_lb_cid_config`] tears the installed context back\n19\t//!   down (no-op when none is installed).\n20\t//! * [`ConnectionIdContext::generate`] / [`ConnectionIdContext::verify`]\n21\t//!   are the per-CID callback bodies.  They are exposed as inherent methods\n22\t//!   so the eventual [`crate::ConnectionIdCallback`] trait impl can\n23\t//!   delegate to them; the C `void* connection_id_cb_data` parameter is\n24\t//!   recovered as `&mut self`.\n25\t//!\n26\t//! ## Field-shape decisions\n27\t//!\n28\t//! * `rotation_bits : 2` — multi-bit C bitfield, kept as `u8`\n29\t//!   (legal range `0..=3`) per the Phase 1 bitfield rule.\n30\t//! * `first_byte_encodes_length : 1` — single-bit C bitfield used\n31\t//!   as a Boolean flag throughout the C body, promoted to `bool`\n32\t//!   (matching the `config.rs` precedent).\n33\t//! * `cid_encryption_context` / `cid_decryption_context` were\n34\t//!   `void*` in C, holding AES-128-ECB contexts produced by\n35\t//!   `aes128_ecb_create` and freed by `aes128_ecb_free` (both\n36\t//!   declared in `tls_api.h`, which has not been translated yet).\n37\t//!   The fields own their contexts in C (the clear path explicitly\n38\t//!   calls the AES destructor), so Rust models them as\n39\t//!   `Option<Box<Aes128EcbContext>>`, with the AES-context type\n40\t//!   forward-declared here as an opaque struct.  `Box::drop` will\n41\t//!   subsume the explicit `aes128_ecb_free` call once Phase 3 gives\n42\t//!   the type a `Drop` impl.\n43\t//! * `server_id: [u8; 16]` and `cid_encryption_key: [u8; 16]` stay\n44\t//!   as fixed-capacity byte arrays — the C declarations are size-16\n45\t//!   arrays and the algorithms are AES-128, so the capacity *is*\n46\t//!   part of the contract.\n47\t\n48\tuse crate::Error;\n49\tuse crate::tls_api::Aes128EcbContext;\n50\tuse crate::{ConnectionId, Quic};\n51\t\n52\t/// CID-encoding method selected by the load-balancer config.\n53\t/// C: `load_balancer_cid_method_enum`.\n54\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n55\tpub enum ConnectionIdMethod {\n56\t    /// Server ID copied in clear after the first byte.\n57\t    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_clear`.\n58\t    #[default]\n59\t    Clear,\n60\t    /// Server ID encrypted with an AES-128-ECB stream cipher keyed\n61\t    /// off a per-CID nonce.\n62\t    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_stream_cipher`.\n63\t    StreamCipher,\n64\t    /// Server ID encrypted with a single AES-128-ECB block.\n65\t    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_block_cipher`.\n66\t    BlockCipher,\n67\t}\n68\t\n69\t/// Number of low bits of the first CID byte that select between\n70\t/// short-lived configurations.  C: `unsigned int rotation_bits : 2`.\n71\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n72\t#[repr(u8)]\n73\tpub enum RotationBits {\n74\t    #[default]\n75\t    Zero = 0,\n76\t    One = 1,\n77\t    Two = 2,\n78\t    Three = 3,\n79\t}\n80\t\n81\t/// Configuration parsed from an LB-supplied string and applied to\n82\t/// a [`Quic`] via [`Quic::set_lb_cid_config`].\n83\t//