{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_014rFdR9gTzFxEeXWKmM41w3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at `tests/socket.rs`, it calls:\n- `crate::socks::ServerAddress::resolve` — needs implementation\n- `ServerSockets<Socket2Udp>` — needs `open` etc.\n- It already uses `crate::socks_socket2::Socket2Udp` concretely\n\nThe test file mentions `socket_ping_pong` is `todo!(\"socket_ping_pong\")`. So the test itself doesn't exercise all functions.\n\nFor `ServerSockets::open`, the C code creates sockets. In Rust with `S: Socket`, there's no generic `open`. But the test calls `ServerSockets<Socket2Udp>::open(port)`. \n\nThe issue is that `open` is defined as generic over `S: Socket`, but you can't instantiate `S` without knowing the concrete type — there's no factory mechanism. This looks like a design problem in the stub that would need either specialization or a factory trait to actually work.\n\nLooking at the test, `server_sockets` is passed in as an already-constructed `ServerSockets<Socket2Udp>`, which means `open` must have been called somewhere else to set it up. Let me trace through the full test to see where that happens.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5913,"cache_read_input_tokens":160141,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5913},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0ef721e-e3c5-4ce3-b9f2-772ba297c48d","uuid":"98ad1188-c766-4984-ada2-cb7144dccf58"}
